<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- aps-environment, and nothing else.

	     This file was an empty dict until now, while project.yml said in
	     a comment that the entitlement was in it. On the simulator that
	     is invisible: the simulator has no APNs, and Push.swift's
	     registration is never exercised there. On a real phone
	     `registerForRemoteNotifications` fails with "no valid
	     aps-environment entitlement string found", which reads as a
	     provisioning problem and is a missing key.

	     `development`, not `production`, and that is deliberate: Xcode
	     replaces this value with `production` when it re-signs during
	     `-exportArchive` for App Store distribution, so a TestFlight
	     build gets a production token and a build run from Xcode onto
	     the same handset gets a sandbox one. Writing `production` here
	     would break the second without helping the first.

	     Which environment a token belongs to is sent to the Pi
	     alongside it - see the note at the top of Push.swift. A sandbox
	     token offered to the production APNs host comes back
	     `BadDeviceToken`, which reads as malformed rather than as
	     addressed to the wrong Apple. -->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
